A class defines two member functions with bodies:

void save(int x) const { /* A */ }
void save(const int x) const { /* B */ }

where the const in the second declaration qualifies the parameter x itself, not anything it points to or refers to. What happens when this class is compiled?

What is the outcome of compiling this?

struct Base {
  virtual void step() final {}
};
struct Derived : Base {
  void step() {}
};

Given:

struct Base {
  void show(int) {}
  void show(double) {}
};
struct Derived : Base {
  using Base::show;
  void show(const char*) {}
};

Derived d; Which of these is true of d.show(3), d.show(3.5) and d.show("hi")?

Given:

struct Base {
  virtual void notify() {}
  void notify(int n) {}
};
struct Derived : Base {
  void notify() override {}
};

Derived d; Base* bp = &d; Derived* dp = &d; Which call fails to compile?

Rec is defined as struct Rec { char data[10]; }; (size exactly 10, no padding). The file recs.bin is exactly 25 bytes long. What does this fragment print, and what is the resulting state of arr[2]?

ifstream f("recs.bin", ios::binary);
Rec arr[3];
f.read(reinterpret_cast<char*>(arr), sizeof(arr));
cout << f.gcount() << ' ' << (f.gcount() / sizeof(Rec));

